# Commands

Ten commands, one vocabulary. Every one of them works in a `//` group opener
and on a `/cmd()` line, and they combine on the same opener — except
`caption`, which only ever means something on a `//` group. `caption` and
`snug` both **backward-attach**, binding to the block immediately *before*
them rather than wrapping what follows, and — wanting the same preceding
block — can't combine with *each other* either. `caption`'s position
argument implies deliberate figure/figcaption boundaries a `/cmd()`
directive (which only ever wraps forward) can't express, so it's `//`-group
only; `snug` has no such argument, so `/snug()` works too, as long as it's
the first command in its chain (the one whose wrapped block actually reaches
a preceding sibling to pop). [Groups](groups.sx) covers the grammar those
lines are written in; this page is what you can put in them.

| Command | Argument | Bare form |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `grid(n)` | column count, n ≥ 1 | — |
| `skinny(N%)` | 1–100 | 75% |
| `wide(N%)` | 101–200 | 125% |
| `center()` | none | — |
| `color(role)` | `accent`, `muted`, `fg` | — |
| `collapse()` | nothing, or `open` | closed |
| `indent(n)` | n ≥ 1 | one step |
| `citations()` | none | — |
| `caption()` / `caption(pos)` | `top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right` | bottom |
| `caption(left\|right, N%)` | split percent, 1–99 | 30% |
| `snug()` | none | — |

## grid

`grid(n)` arranges a group's sections in n columns; sections fill left to right
and wrap.

// three grid(3)

**One**

Narrow columns want short content.

// --

**Two**

Three across is about the limit at the default reading width.

// --

**Three**

A `grid(n)` opened with the wrong number of sections still renders every
section — it just warns.

// end three

`grid(1)` is legal and does exactly what it says: one column, which is a way to
give a run of content a container without changing its arrangement.

// single grid(1)

One column. The group is real, the layout is a no-op, and any other command on
the opener still applies.

// end single

## skinny and wide

One width control with two names, meeting at 100%. `skinny` takes a percentage
below it, `wide` above, and both center what they hold.

/skinny(60%)

60% of the body column.

/skinny()

The bare form: `skinny()` with no argument, which is 75%.

// wide_demo wide(115%)

This group bleeds past the reading column on both sides — useful for a wide
table or a figure that suffocates at the body width.

| a wide table | column two | column three | column four |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| room to breathe | without | squeezing | the text |

// end wide_demo

/wide()

And the bare form of the other one: `wide()`, which is 125%.

Written on the same opener they do not stack — they are one setting under two
names, so the last one wins.

## center

`center()` centers the text within whatever contains it, with no argument.

/center()

Centered relative to the body column.

// centered_narrow skinny(50%) center()

Centered *within* a skinny group, which is itself centered in the body. The two
commands are independent, which is why they combine.

// end centered_narrow

## color

`color(role)` sets a theme **role** — never a concrete color — so colored text
follows the reader's theme. There are three, and all three are here.

// accent_block color(accent)

**accent** — the theme's signature color. Try the season buttons in Settings and
watch this paragraph change with the rest of the page.

// end accent_block

// muted_block color(muted)

**muted** — secondary text, for asides and marginal notes like this one.

Roles nest and the inner one wins, so [`fg` reads as a reset].color(fg) back to
the body color from inside a muted region.

// end muted_block

Inline, the same command marks a run mid-sentence: an
[accent phrase].color(accent), a [muted phrase].color(muted), and an
[fg phrase].color(fg) all in one paragraph.

The bracketed text is fully parsed, so a span can hold
[**bold**, `code`, and $x^2$].color(accent) alike. What it cannot hold is a
link: `[label](url).color(accent)` is a link followed by literal text, because
the link form wins the bracket.

## collapse

`collapse()` folds a group behind its **leader** — the first content element,
which stays visible and becomes the control.

// faq collapse()

**`collapse()` folds a group behind its first element**

Everything after the leader folds away until the reader opens it.

Other commands on the same group apply to the folded body, not the leader.

// end faq

// faq_open collapse(open)

**`collapse(open)` is the same fold, starting expanded**

Use it when the body is the point and the fold is a courtesy — the reader can
still collapse it, but nothing is hidden on arrival.

// end faq_open

A group with fewer than two content elements has no leader to show, so it
renders an anonymous bar instead and folds everything beneath it:

// bar collapse()

// --

The whole of this group's content lives in its second section, so the first has
nothing to lift into a leader. The bar above is what that looks like.

// end bar

Open and closed are properties of the reading, not the document — nothing
persists, and a renderer with no interactivity should show these expanded.

## indent

`indent(n)` is a typographic tab: it insets an element's **first line**, not its
whole box.

/indent()

The bare form is one step. This paragraph's first line is inset and the rest of
it wraps flush against the body's left edge, which is what a tab indent means in
typesetting and what a margin shift does not.

/indent(3)

Three steps deep, same rule — first line only.

  Leading whitespace on a paragraph is sugar for exactly one step, so this
  paragraph needs no directive at all. Only the first line is read, which is why
  this second line is an ordinary continuation rather than a second indent.

On a **group**, indent reaches each content element separately — every paragraph
gets its own first-line inset, rather than the group moving as one block:

// indented_group indent(2)

The first paragraph of an indented group, inset two steps on its first line.

The second paragraph, inset the same way. The group is not a shifted box; each
element inside it is indented in its own right.

// end indented_group

A **list** is the exception, and it is the interesting one. A list owns its
marker column, and that column is part of the element rather than part of its
text — so an indented list moves as a whole, markers and items together:

/indent(2)

- an indented list
- moves as one box
  - and a sublist inside it is not shifted again, because its parent already
    moved
- so the numbers and bullets travel with their items

/indent(2)

1. the same is true of an ordered list
2. where a first-line inset would otherwise land *between* the number and the
   item, which is plainly wrong

Compare that with the paragraph indents further up: same command, same meaning,
two realizations, because the elements are not alike. Which command means what
on which element type is tracked in the reference docs — most pairs are still
open, and this one is decided.

Being non-layout, `indent` nests by scope rather than stacking: an inner value
overrides the inherited one for its own subtree.

## citations

`citations()` declares that a group's numbered list is the document's
bibliography. It gets its own page — [Citations](citations.sx) — because the
other half of the feature is an inline form.

## caption

A `caption` group wraps the element *before* it in a `<figure>`, with its own
content as the `<figcaption>` — a real caption, not a paragraph that merely
sits nearby ([Images](images.sx) has the before/after and every position).

![the strike logo](logo.svg)

// caption()
The strike wordmark.
// end

A sibling command on the same opener applies to the whole figure, caption
included:

![the strike logo](logo.svg)

// skinny(50%) caption(top)
Narrowed, captioned above instead of below — still one figure.
// end

`left`/`right` put the caption beside the image instead, sized by a split
percent:

![the strike logo](logo.svg)

// caption(left, 35%)
A left-side caption, sitting at the bottom of the column when it's shorter
than the image.
// end

## snug

`snug()` is the other backward-attach command: same binding as `caption`,
but with no figure, no position, no argument — its only job is closing the
gap between a block and the one right after it, for pairs that read as one
unit split in two. It pops its partner without ever restyling it: a command
chained alongside `snug()` describes the snug body only.

### A heading with a tightly bound subtitle

// snug()
The subtitle sits close to the heading above it, instead of at ordinary
paragraph distance.
// end

Unlike `caption`, `snug` also works as a `/cmd()` single-command directive —
as long as it's the first command in its chain, so its wrapped block
actually reaches the sibling above to pop:

### Another heading

/snug()
Same result, single-command form.

Chaining a style onto either form colors (or sizes, or anything else) only
the attached content — never the heading it's attached to:

### A third heading, left untouched

// color(accent) snug()
Only this subtitle takes the accent color.
// end

Chaining `/snug()` with another single-command line works the same way —
`/snug()` has to lead the chain (it's the token that actually reaches the
heading above to pop); what follows nests inside it, same as any other
`/cmd()` chain:

### A fourth heading, still untouched

/snug()
/color(muted)
Same result again, chained single-command form.

Because it wants the same preceding block a caption would pop, `snug()`
can't combine with `caption()` on one opener — that combination degrades
the whole line to prose, same as any other malformed argument:

// snug() caption()

This line and its group never form — see the raw source, not a figure.

// end

## Combining

Commands combine on one opener, and the group is the intersection of all of
them:

// combined grid(2) skinny(85%) color(muted)

**A narrow, muted grid**

Three commands on one opener: two columns, 85% of the body width, muted text.

// --

**Nothing special about the combination**

Each command writes its own setting. They interact only where they genuinely
overlap, which is why `skinny` and `wide` are the one pair that cannot usefully
combine.

// end combined
